  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   - Central Idea: Represent the step function as a binary vector derived from a Costas array-like structure, which naturally minimizes self-orthogonality. This approach differentiates from gradient-based methods by leveraging combinatorial optimization principles.\n   - Why it could work: Costas arrays minimize correlation at all shifts, aligning perfectly with our objective of minimizing the overlap integral. The binary representation simplifies the optimization landscape while still being able to converge to near-optimal solutions.\n   - Bottleneck Addressed: This method tackles local optima issues head-on by using global search via local perturbations and simulated annealing, guided by the Costas array structure.\n   - Limitations: The combinatorial explosion means we can only handle practical resolutions up to ~1000; however, the cost of evaluation is lower than continuous methods.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   - Representation: Use a binary vector where 1's represent active regions of h(x). Constraint: Exactly half of the vector must be 1's (h(x)*dx=1).\n   - Variables: The binary decisions about which regions are active.\n   - Objective: Min max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   - Constraints: \u2211h(x) = n/2 (binary) -> exactly n/2 ones\n   - Optimization: Define quality by the maximum overlap at any shift.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Start with parent-based initialization if provided\n   - Define neighbor functions:\n     * Local moves: single flips, adjacent swaps, 2-opt moves\n     * Global perturbation: Random flipping pattern matching a Costas array\n   - Fitness: Using `compute_all_g` to get overlap at all shifts\n   - Annealing schedule:\n     * Start with high temperature, slowly decrease\n     * Maintain global best solution\n   - Repair: When sum != n/2, use minimum adjustment (since binary, easier than continuous)\n   - Representation: Binary vector of appropriate length\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   - Default: Single flip mutation with Costas pattern perturbation\n   - Temperature schedule: Fast cooling initially, slower near solutions\n   - Multiple tries of best-known blocks\n   - Improvement gauge: Successive record keeping\n   - Variations to try:\n     * 2-point swaps\n     * Simulated tempering\n     * Multiple parent crossover from Costas array generation\n\n5. Budget allocation and robustness:\n   - 20% for initialization\n   - 70% for iterative refinement\n   - Check module boundaries:\n     * State current best solution\n     * Budget remaining before expensive moves\n   - Resolution up to 2000 points with 50 seconds\n   - Valid fallback: Current parent or last best solution\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Check final_h has exactly n/2 ones\n   - Compute objective exactly against evaluator\n   - Return if against boundaries\n   - Explicit repair ensures binary and integral constraints\n   - Format as (h_values, c5_value, n_points)",
